# EXPECT_RUNTIME_ERROR
# JIT_DISABLE
# reason: with every method compiled, the exception this test throws inside the interpreter callback cannot unwind through the JIT'd frame and obr aborts with SIGABRT before Execute's catch, its message and its return value (a separate follow-up); this test is about the entry point's exit status after that catch
#~
A program that dies inside the VM must leave obr with a non-zero exit status.

Execute (core/vm/vm.cpp) catches an exception that escapes the interpreter,
prints ">>> virtual machine: internal error: ... <<<" and returns -1. The
POSIX entry point (core/vm/posix_main.cpp) discarded that result and returned
0, so on Linux and macOS such a program reported success to whatever ran it --
a shell, a Makefile, a CI step -- and only a reader of stderr knew. Windows
returned the -1.

The way in is "1e999"->ToFloat(): std::stod throws out_of_range, and the
interpreter's Str2Float catches invalid_argument only, so the exception
escapes to Execute on every platform and in every VM mode (the JIT hands S2F
back to the interpreter).

THE TRIGGER IS ITSELF A KNOWN BUG. ToFloat on an out-of-range literal should
yield a value, not kill the program; its fix is tracked as a task of its own.
Nothing in the VM raises an exception on purpose, so any fixture for this
catch rides on an unguarded one. Whoever fixes ToFloat must give this file
another trigger that escapes Execute as a std::exception (anything that lands
in the catch printing "internal error"); otherwise this file exits 0 and fails
as # EXPECT_RUNTIME_ERROR -- the regression runner will say "should have
failed at runtime" and run_vm_flag_tests.py (section 7) will name the missing
message. The case first seen, on macOS, was Runtime->SetProperty("locale",
"xx_YY.bogus") dying with "locale constructed with null"; #774 makes that call
survive, so it is not used here.

The regression runner passes this file only on a non-zero exit WITH output,
which is exactly the fix: the old obr printed the line and exited 0.

The JIT opt-out marker above: with every method compiled on its first call
(OBJECK_JIT_THRESHOLD=1, --jit=1) the exception is thrown inside the
interpreter callback that the compiled Main made, and it cannot unwind
through the JIT'd frame: libc++ terminates the process (SIGABRT, "terminating
due to uncaught exception") before Execute's catch, its message and its return
value are reached. That is a defect of its own, in the JIT rather than the
entry point, and it would let the runner's second pass count an abort as this
test's expected error; the marker keeps both passes on the path this file is
about. Observed on ARM64 (2026-09-10); the AMD64 backend registers no unwind
information for its code either.
~#

class VmErrorExit {
	function : Main(args : String[]) ~ Nil {
		"before"->PrintLine();
		"1e999"->ToFloat()->PrintLine();
		"after"->PrintLine();
	}
}
